  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nMostly Dry This Week...The region will be under the influence of a \nridge of high pressure through at least Friday, keeping conditions \ndry under mostly sunny skies. Guidance indicates the next chance for \nrain sometime this weekend as a mid-level trough and surface low \neject northeast from the Middle Rockies. Any precip will be \nassociated with the surface low's cold front as it sweeps across the \nUpper Mississippi Valley, with the earliest guidance starting late \nSaturday night and with the latest guidance exiting on Monday. \nHowever, forcing and moisture is rather lacking due the cold front \nbecoming too far removed from the surface low. This leaves the \nforecast area with low chances for measurable precip (0.01\") of less \nthan 25%.\n\nFog Potential...The prevailing high pressure combined with nighttime \ncooling and light winds will result in the potential for at least \npatchy ground fog each night/morning from tonight through Thursday \nnight. Patchy dense fog may occur during these times.\n\nTemperatures...This weather pattern will keep above normal \ntemperatures in the forecast through the weekend with highs mainly \nin the 60s each day. A few spots in central and far northeast WI may \nreach into the low 70s a couple of the days, from Wednesday through \nSunday, as NBM probs for 70 degrees or warmer sit around 30-50% \neach day. After tonight's low temps in the low 30s to low 40s, \novernight lows will be rise above normal through the weekend, with\nvalues mainly in the 40s.",
